1473241927867695228,1300553352274382848,ذ・bitsec・60,799672011265015819,1199774337658519572,lord_ciux21,Lord_ciux21,"The core issue was not the IM itself. While the initial version was admittedly vague and contained gaps and blind spots, those shortcomings were addressed in the subsequent release. The primary problem instead stemmed from governance and decision-making under pressure.

In the first release, one of the stated conditions was that solutions should not be hardcoded, based on the understanding that hardcoded approaches tend to generalize poorly. However, after the results were published, significant pressure emerged from a competing miner team that placed second and raised allegations of hardcoding against the top solution. Independent evaluation on an additional project dataset — outside the direct challenge — showed that the top miner’s solution generalized better than the competing approach. Despite acknowledging this, the owner chose to revise the original enforcement stance and reallocated emissions away from the top-performing miner. This established a precedent that persistent complaints and fear-driven narratives could influence outcomes.

In the second release, the competition rules were defined more clearly. Nevertheless, because prior pressure tactics had proven effective, the same approach was repeated by dissatisfied participants. Although the owner ultimately upheld the formal rules after extensive discussion, alternative rule interpretations proposed by competing miners were still entertained, which again introduced uncertainty and dispute.",2026-02-17T08:57:27.115000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,0
1473241953897549937,1300553352274382848,ذ・bitsec・60,799672011265015819,1199774337658519572,lord_ciux21,Lord_ciux21,"For the third challenge, the rules were initially made even more explicit. However, during the competition additional participation and evaluation criteria were introduced that had not been disclosed beforehand and were not applied consistently across all miners. These additions were communicated in subjective terms — for example, dissatisfaction with a solution that achieved the highest score on the pre-competition tests — without clearly defined objective thresholds or measurable standards. Mid-competition rule and evaluation changes of this nature created further ambiguity around fairness and process integrity.

This repeated pattern of modifying rules and evaluation criteria after competitions had already started led to renewed conflict and ultimately contributed to the decision to halt emissions.

In winner-takes-all competitive environments, participants are strongly incentivized to advocate aggressively for their position. The most reliable way to ensure fairness and stability is to define rules clearly in advance and apply them consistently throughout the competition. Repeated deviations from pre-established rules undermined trust in the process and are the primary reason the subnet is now burning emissions. Other factors are secondary by comparison.",2026-02-17T08:57:33.321000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,0
