The sparket patent discusses sourcing odds from a crowd-sourced mechanism as a combination of user bet data, parimutuel lines, and more concrete probabilistic/machine learning analysis over a wider field of input data like historical performance, rosters, injuries, weather, and anything else you can think of which might impact the outcome of a sporting event. **This is where bittensor, and the miners come in**: Our current incentive mechanism is designed to select for outputs which contribute positive value to our aggregate - so we don't necessarily need sharps or geniuses to give us the good stuff, we can build effective, well priced odds out of a good enough aggregate. This is data that is directly valuable to **current clients of Sparket**, and also to products that Sparket is still developing.

Once we have worked out the initial integration pipeline and the miners outputs have risen to an acceptable standard, we will send this data feed to the sparket aggregator/ system. In that pipeline, I am sure we can find a way to prove that our outputs are going somewhere without exposing anything proprietary. Fear not, that is our core priority at this moment.

