1475729980414754896,1381987595881414656,ⴷ・ⴷffine・120,799672011265015819,1279089539536654480,ntro_manager,Chris,"Let me remind the Affine history for those who frequently forget (or pretend to forget) it.
The old days, when most envs are comparatively straightforward and genuine Q/A style, nothing can beat overfit small models.
But later, team released more advanced challenges, and as a result, the sizes of models started to increase ~ 4B, 8B, 15B, 30B, ...
The first env that firstly broke the trend was PRINT. All found that it's beyond what even overfitting can handle easily.
It was a natural choice of miners to give their eyes on large open source models.
Those giant models were not able to be the top for basic envs (even now open source models are pretty much worse in LGC v2), but exceled in PRINT.
As SWE SYNTH and Live Web emerged, the power of large LLMs were highlighted more.
However, at this moment, we're seeing that there's no infinite number of large open source LLMs in the world, and, their scores have a lot of room to improve.
So what now? Simple. Just go as we used to go. Compete to improve scores. That's it.
Everything was a natural progress more than what we can expect from Affine-like ecosystems.
Team allowed overfit for early phase, increase the difficulty of challenges, miners try to adopt to it, and now there is a big open world to overcome where overfitting won't work any more.
But some people are insisting the walkback. What they want is to mislead the team to fit them, not to try themselves to fit the vision.
We're just at the beginning of new game. No human can build a workable model in a few weeks.
If you can't train large models, please sit in a chair and watch others do, instead of asking evidence or something like that jumping the gun up.",2026-02-24T05:44:05.068000+00:00,2026-02-24T07:21:03.937000+00:00,False,,False,0,0,1
