1476938608618242068,1397618038894759956,ץ・resi・46,799672011265015819,1185184083937079339,jordun01,Jordun01,"Thanks for clarifying.

I understand the idea behind exponential decay, but I’m a bit concerned about the incentives it creates.

With a 100 → 50 → 25 → 12.5 structure, revenue drops very quickly. Instead of focusing purely on improving a strong model, miners may start optimizing deployment strategy — for example, holding back slightly better versions and releasing them sequentially just to reset the reward cycle.

That shifts the focus from building the best possible model to managing timing and reward mechanics.

Also, if someone is genuinely Top 1 and no one can beat the model, it means they are providing the highest value to the subnet at that time. In that case, the natural incentive should be to keep earning while continuing to improve — because their main goal is to stay on top and not get overtaken.

Strong competition already pushes continuous development. Rapid exponential decay may unintentionally reward strategic resets more than real innovation.",2026-02-27T13:46:44.487000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,0
