1476978074074742988,1397618038894759956,ץ・resi・46,799672011265015819,1185184083937079339,jordun01,Jordun01,"Seby, just to expand on the 82% → 76% point.

Our models are continuously adapting to new data. When the data distribution evolves, improvements are not always immediately reflected in higher scores. Sometimes there’s a temporary dip while the model adjusts, followed by more stable and gradual improvements.

Especially in a new subnet, some volatility is natural. Strong models require proper retraining cycles, and meaningful progress takes time. Training is expensive — it requires high-end hardware, significant compute, and multiple days of work. Because of that, improvements tend to be incremental rather than instant jumps.

That’s also why a stable and fair reward structure is important. Sustainable earnings allow miners to reinvest into better hardware, more data, and longer training cycles. Without that, it becomes difficult to focus on long-term model quality and innovation.",2026-02-27T16:23:33.785000+00:00,,False,1476969138848137216,False,0,0,2
