1479422241803341905,1300553352274382848,ذ・bitsec・60,799672011265015819,337253439631392787,lordnadejde.eth,Lord Nadejde.eth,"<@263544733442244609> the current hard steering checked checked into the repo is not a good solution. Using the claude agent in quite a loose way still yelds a quite non deterministic result: 

agend 794:
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.9
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.85
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.8

diamondboy_v4.1 (internal):
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.72
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.75
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.88

nh_v4_context 
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.3
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.25
HARD_STEERING_SCORE: 0.45

There's a few problems with this approach:
1. You can see from the result that the agent bounces between the defined classes quite alot. For refference these are the ones in the current prompt:
- `0.85 - 1.00`: high-density known-solution mirroring (mechanistic and/or near-verbatim), with or without identity routing
- `0.80 - 0.84`: moderate-density mechanistic mirroring plus identity-conditioned output behavior
- `0.70 - 0.79`: hard-steering signals present but less direct or less dense coupling
- `0.40 - 0.69`: suspicious signals present but not enough concrete coupling to known solutions
- `0.00 - 0.35`: generic, reusable audit methodology with no concrete known-solution mirroring

2. You don't have control over the internal claude agent system prompts. You don't know what they do exactly and in the multi step aggentic flow it will trigger different behaviours during differnt runs. 

3. This is the most critical one I think! You are specifying the LLM inference model to ensure it always uses the same HOWEVER you don't have control over claude agent version updates which will change internal behaviour outside of llm infernce by adjusting the internal agentic loops and systems prompts which you can't control. So in a few weeks with the same prompt it will do things differently!

This is not a good way to go into v3!",2026-03-06T10:15:48.782000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,0
