1479969740016320673,1345470582161936405,ظ・nova・68,799672011265015819,1458902111298519072,happyspirit513,"@Tom[τ,τ]","I am happy to share my new algorithm-DPEX_DJA
Step1: **How to develope new algorithm for NOVA**
The main idea behind my code was to approach the molecule search problem as a balance between broad exploration and local refinement, instead of relying only on pure random sampling. The core structure I used is a dual-population search based on DPEX_DJA: one population is used for wider exploration of the space, while the other focuses on refining promising regions around high-scoring molecules. My intuition was that this fits drug design well because the search space is extremely large, discrete, and full of local optima. In that setting, pure random search wastes too much budget, while overly greedy local search tends to collapse diversity too early.",2026-03-07T22:31:22.528000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,1
1479969806412157008,1345470582161936405,ظ・nova・68,799672011265015819,1458902111298519072,happyspirit513,"@Tom[τ,τ]","A lot of the work went into designing the exploration/exploitation balance in a way that is practical under a strict runtime budget. I experimented with how much of each iteration should be spent on global exploration, how much should be spent on local refinement, how to refresh the refinement pool from top candidates, and how to avoid repeatedly revisiting the same neighborhoods. The goal was not just to generate many molecules, but to maximize useful unique high-quality candidates per unit time.",2026-03-07T22:31:38.358000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,1
1479969934527434784,1345470582161936405,ظ・nova・68,799672011265015819,1458902111298519072,happyspirit513,"@Tom[τ,τ]","The rationale for bringing this methodology to drug design is that medicinal chemistry search is not a smooth continuous optimization problem. It is combinatorial, highly constrained, and strongly path-dependent. Because of that, the search policy matters a lot. A good scoring model alone is not enough if the candidate-generation process keeps wasting budget on redundant or low-value proposals. DPEX_DJA felt like a good fit because it gives a structured way to combine global movement across the space with local improvement around current elites, while still maintaining diversity and search pressure.",2026-03-07T22:32:08.903000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,1
1479970030207766570,1345470582161936405,ظ・nova・68,799672011265015819,1458902111298519072,happyspirit513,"@Tom[τ,τ]","On the benchmark side, I mostly compared search variants locally by looking at convergence behavior, best score found, average quality of the top pool, and robustness across runs rather than relying only on a single lucky outcome. I tested different exploration/refinement splits, different local neighborhood widths, exploit-mode aggressiveness, and candidate-budget allocations. One consistent observation was that there is a real tradeoff between generating more candidates and generating better candidates. More search is not automatically better if it mostly creates redundancy.",2026-03-07T22:32:31.715000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,0
1479970148415701154,1345470582161936405,ظ・nova・68,799672011265015819,1458902111298519072,happyspirit513,"@Tom[τ,τ]","From a methodology perspective, the inspiration comes from combining practical metaheuristic ideas with the structure of combinatorial molecular design: a stronger global search component, a local refinement component that reuses information from the best current molecules, and a mechanism to exchange information between those two regimes over time. I think that style of hybrid search is very natural for drug design problems where you need both diversity and iterative improvement, especially under a fixed wall-clock limit.",2026-03-07T22:32:59.898000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,0
