1479978564127559770,1345470582161936405,ظ・nova・68,799672011265015819,1458902111298519072,happyspirit513,"@Tom[τ,τ]","Thanks. My intuition is that this kind of hybrid method should become even more useful when the scoring function gets more expensive.
If scoring is cheap, you can afford to waste some budget on broader search or redundant candidates. But when scoring becomes expensive, the main problem changes: it is no longer only about exploring a huge space, it is about sending only the most useful candidates to the scorer. That is exactly where a DPEX_DJA-style hybrid helps. The DJA side is there to keep global exploration alive and prevent the search from collapsing too early, while the Tabu side is there to locally refine around promising regions and avoid repeatedly revisiting the same moves. So the hybrid is meant to improve candidate efficiency, not just candidate volume.",2026-03-07T23:06:26.360000+00:00,,False,1479972811303817277,False,0,0,1
