1479978745908433007,1345470582161936405,ظ・nova・68,799672011265015819,1458902111298519072,happyspirit513,"@Tom[τ,τ]","For something like Boltz-2 scoring, I would expect the search loop to shift even more toward selective generation. In that setting I would probably reduce raw candidate breadth, make the local refinement branch more conservative, and spend more effort on filtering, diversity control, and prioritization before scoring. The higher the scoring cost, the more important it becomes that every scored molecule has a real reason to be evaluated. So I think the value of this methodology is not only that it can search broadly, but that it can organize search pressure into global exploration plus local exploitation under a fixed evaluation budget.",2026-03-07T23:07:09.700000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,1
1479979052839211112,1345470582161936405,ظ・nova・68,799672011265015819,1458902111298519072,happyspirit513,"@Tom[τ,τ]","So overall, I would expect the same framework to remain useful, but with different operating parameters:
fewer total scored candidates
stronger candidate filtering before scoring
more emphasis on diversity and non-redundancy
more selective local refinement around strong regions
more careful allocation between global and local search
In short, I think hybrid search becomes more attractive as scoring gets more expensive, because the bottleneck moves from “how many molecules can I generate?” to “how do I choose the right molecules to spend scoring budget on?”",2026-03-07T23:08:22.878000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,1
