> “SN118 can be thought of as a common-good subnet for the Alpha market: it builds better trading infrastructure outside the pools. That kind of infrastructure needs a funding mechanism—so HODL charges a small, explicit fee. The key is that this ‘tax’ is designed to be cheaper than the implicit pool tax (slippage/price impact) you’d otherwise pay.”",2026-03-09T07:11:05.373000+00:00,2026-03-09T07:34:31.209000+00:00,False,,False,0,0,0
1480462951893696513,1381659575522033724,ⲁ・hodl-eτf・118,799672011265015819,318932875686707210,spacetime_tao,Spaceτime,"""Why does it need a funding mechanism?""

> Because the thing SN118 is trying to provide—reliable liquidity and good off-pool trading—doesn’t happen for free.
> 
> You need trading fees for:
>     •    IMMs + infrastructure: Even though SN118 is no-compute, the system still has real costs. IMMs incur automation/bot upkeep, chain transaction fees, and inventory/risk management (capital + volatility). On top of that, the exchange needs ongoing ops to keep escrow settlement and the orderbook reliable. In short: the fee funds a trading desk + market infrastructure, not GPUs.
> 
> Without a funding mechanism, you either get:
>     •    a dead market (no makers, slow fills), or
>     •    a fully centralized operator subsidizing it out of pocket (not sustainable).
> 
> So the fee is the system’s way to make the infrastructure self-sustaining—and the pitch is that it replaces a worse “funding mechanism” users already pay today: pool slippage/price impact.",2026-03-09T07:11:13.403000+00:00,2026-03-09T16:58:44.686000+00:00,False,,False,0,0,0
