1480529660214378651,1381659575522033724,ⲁ・hodl-eτf・118,799672011265015819,318932875686707210,spacetime_tao,Spaceτime,"the most exciting thing about this metric:

> MEP traces back to Chutes’ Novelty Search framework; I translated that idea into a simple benchmark:
> 
> MEP = Daily net trading fees (TAO) ÷ Daily miner Alpha emissions.
> 
> Here’s the key insight: each Alpha halving cuts daily miner emissions in half.
> So if net trading fees stayed flat—say the exchange keeps generating 30 TAO/day—then MEP mechanically rises.
>     •    Before halving: 30 / 2,952 ≈ 0.01 TAO
>     •    After 1st halving (emissions halve): 30 / 1,476 ≈ 0.02 TAO
>     •    After 2nd halving: 30 / 738 ≈ 0.04 TAO
> 
> So even under the conservative assumption that fees don’t grow, the support benchmark doubles each halving because the same fee revenue is being matched against a smaller stream of daily emissions.
> 
> And that’s the bigger thesis: if net fees do grow over time as off-pool trading becomes more important, then MEP can rise even faster. The other nice part is the operating profile: compared to compute-heavy subnets, this model can scale revenue with relatively low operating overhead.",2026-03-09T11:36:17.906000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,1
