Biggest “OpEx” for SN118 IMMs (what it actually costs to run a market maker)
> Even though SN118 is no-compute, being an Incentivized Market Maker isn’t free. The main costs aren’t GPUs—they’re operational and capital-related:
> Automation (bots/scripts): IMMs will almost certainly run software that watches the orderbook/escrow events and fills orders automatically. That means dev time to build/adapt a bot for Subtensor + escrow mechanics, plus ongoing maintenance as the exchange evolves.
> 
> Chain transaction fees: Every meaningful action (create/fill/settle, and any cancel/update flows) costs on-chain fees. If an IMM is high-frequency, this becomes a real operating line item; reactive, selective filling keeps it lower.
> 
> Inventory management (the real economic cost): IMMs need TAO and Alpha inventory to make markets. Managing that inventory—avoiding one-sided exposure, handling volatility, and not getting picked off when prices move—is where most of the “cost” lives (risk + opportunity cost of capital), even if the subnet itself requires no compute.
> 
> Bottom line: SN118 replaces hardware OpEx with automation + chain fees + inventory/risk management—more like running a trading desk than running a data center.",2026-03-10T05:11:46.264000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,1
