1480981415620378799,1311109830282313781,ש・synth・50,799672011265015819,582789679431155722,amberz.s,"amberz.synth [τ,τ]","**Subnet Update: Reward Curve Adjustment and UID Trimming**
We will be making two changes to improve competition and overall network quality on Synth in the coming days.

**1. Reward Curve Adjustment**
The reward curve parameter β (currently -0.1) will have a new value.
Recently the rewards curve has become too flat, which reduces competitive pressure among miners. Our goal is to maintain a healthy competitive environment and ensure that truly exceptional miners are properly rewarded.
Several community members have pointed out cases where coldkeys or axons attempted to register a large number of UIDs with mediocre performance. As we have explained before, this strategy does not provide a meaningful advantage. With a steeper reward curve, rewards concentrate more strongly on the best performers. Simply operating many UIDs does not translate into higher emissions, and larger axons do not inherently have an advantage over smaller operators.
Adjusting β will reinforce this principle by ensuring rewards more clearly differentiate between high quality and average predictions.

**2. Reduction of Total Miner Slots (UID Trimming)**
Based on recent miner registration patterns and UID distribution across axons, we will also reduce the total number of available miner slots by 25 using UID trimming. This is intended to discourage UID hoarding.
UID trimming is a subnet management mechanism that allows the subnet owner to reduce the number of registered neuron UIDs while preserving high performing miners.

**Key points about how trimming works:**
• Low emitting UIDs are removed first. UIDs are ranked by emission scores and trimming begins with the lowest performers.
• Immune UIDs are protected. Temporarily immune neurons and subnet owner UIDs cannot be trimmed.
• High performing miners are preserved. The process prioritizes keeping miners that contribute the most emissions.
• UIDs are compressed after trimming so indexing remains consecutive.",2026-03-10T17:31:24.788000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,0
