1481712031357997219,1465796313877577903,ث・niome・55,799672011265015819,1056840005449961482,kabar4573,kabar,"So the validator clearly expects 5 drugs per gene when it scores the task.

 What our miner provides
 We construct a VCF with the requested ABCG2 and CYP3A4 variants (each variant tagged with PX=<gene>). PharmCAT runs cleanly (no warnings/errors), so the input side seems fine.

 What PharmCAT returns
 In the JSON report, genes.ABCG2.relatedDrugs = [""allopurinol"", ""rosuvastatin""],
 and genes.CYP3A4.relatedDrugs = [""quetiapine""].

 None of the other statins listed above show up in relatedDrugs, even though they’re part of the DPWG/FDA panels. To double-check, I even edited the ABCG2 template to force the rs2231142 variant to 1/1—PharmCAT
 still only emits allopurinol + rosuvastatin. So it isn’t a genotype issue on our side; the current PharmCAT build simply doesn’t output the rest of the drugs.

 How the validator scores it
 Because the per-gene lists are shorter than what chr_drugs asked for, the validator subtracts points (pre-fix logic compares lengths and expects every listed drug). The final PharmCAT score for this task is  only 0.30, even though the underlying VCF is correct.

 This pattern repeats across many genes: ABCG2, CYP3A4, CYP3A5, several of the G6PD sets, etc. Until PharmCAT’s drug dictionary lines up with what the task generator is asking for, all miners will get penalized
 simply because the missing drugs never appear in relatedDrugs.

 Could you confirm:
 1. Whether the production task list is supposed to match the current PharmCAT drug catalog?
 2. If so, is there a new PharmCAT image (or forthcoming patch) that restores the missing statin/antibiotic entries?
 3. Otherwise, should we trim our expectations (and chr_drugs) to the smaller subset that PharmCAT actually knows today?

 Thanks!",2026-03-12T17:54:37.154000+00:00,2026-03-12T18:23:47.604000+00:00,False,,False,0,0,0
