I brought up this concept known as miner equilibrium price, which is the price at which revenue, i.e. net trading fees, can offset 100% of daily miner earnings. (formula = revenue (tao) / daily miner alpha emissions). whenever the value of net trading fees is greater than the max potential sell pressure, you get upwards price movement up until the price is equal to MEP. works fairly similar to chain buybacks (when price < emission %, excess tao is used to buy alpha up until price = emission %). it's a theoretical price point because 1) it assumes miners sell 100% of what they earn. and 2) it doesn't take into account what the other holders are doing.

and, here's the other cool thing: they can simply sell their sn118 alpha on the hodl exchange, just like any other alpha. they bypass the pool, and thus bypass slippage and price impact. and you limit outflows. users that would like to acquire the sn118 alpha token can buy it via the hodl exchange. this subnet, and many subnets with revenue, can get away with not having major flows to the pool, because the alpha buybacks are consistent enough to keep the ema flow positive.

last point is that this miner equilibrium price level doubles with each alpha halving. this assumes that revenue remains stagnant over the years. if revenue increases, then MEP rises even further. you have speculation that kickstarts the value of the token (in the early stages i.e. now), but the alpha buybacks give the token ""intrinsic"" value. you can easily gauge the value of the token by looking at the economic output of the subnet. it's not pure speculation driving the price",2026-03-13T09:01:30.248000+00:00,2026-03-13T09:26:10.545000+00:00,False,1481935040085495888,False,0,0,1
