1481943841895616613,1381659575522033724,ⲁ・hodl-eτf・118,799672011265015819,318932875686707210,spacetime_tao,Spaceτime,"a question some people might have, after understanding the concept, is ""why not burn a % of miner emissions to artificially inflate the miner equilibrium price"" answered here

> People ask: **“If you burn Alpha buybacks, why not also burn a % of miner emissions?”**
> 
> Because the buybacks and the emissions do different jobs:
> 
> * **Buyback burns** turn real economic output (net fees) into **circulating supply reduction**.
> * **Miner emissions** are the incentive budget that pays IMMs (**Incentivized Market Makers**) to provide what the subnet is designed for: **reliable market making and better execution outside the subnet pools**.
> 
> If you burn miner emissions, you’re cutting the very incentive that attracts and retains market makers — effectively weakening the execution layer just to make the Miner Equilibrium Price look better. We do not want to manipulate miner emissions to force a higher price. Instead, we keep the incentive system honest: trading generates fees, net fees fund Alpha buybacks (which are then burned), and the market ultimately sets the price through what gets sold versus held.
> 
> Fortunately, unlike compute-heavy subnets, SN118 IMMs have near-zero operating expenses, so forced selling pressure is already much lower. Over time, halvings reduce daily miner emissions, which can mechanically increase the **Miner Equilibrium Price** even under stagnant net fee revenue — giving miners another reason not to sell everything they earn.",2026-03-13T09:15:45.093000+00:00,2026-03-13T09:16:00.493000+00:00,False,1481940885712142410,False,0,0,0
