1482009944545956121,1342559689690583202,ᚱ・vidaio・85,799672011265015819,1247179488337592402,geeboai,g_bo Vidaio,"Thanks everyone for the feedback.

A few clarifications on the proposed change.

First, the adjustment to emissions is not a final design for the subnet, but an interim economic adjustment while we transition the network toward a much larger workload pipeline.

Today, the subnet distributes emissions across 120 miners, but the organic traffic currently routes through the top 10 miners. As a result, a large portion of emissions is being distributed without materially improving throughput or performance.

The goal of the proposed change is therefore to reduce unnecessary emissions while increasing competitive differentiation between miners.

Under the proposal:
 - revise burn emissions to 80% from the current 33%
 - remaining emissions (20% of miner emissions) distributed as follows:
top 10 compression miners: 30% of total miner emissions
top 10 upscaling miners: 20% of total miner emissions
11-50 rank compression miners: 30% of total miner emissions
11-50 rank upscaling miners: 20% of total miner emissions

Importantly, this is stage one of a two-stage upgrade.

Stage 1 is an economic adjustment to reduce wastage while the subnet prepares for significantly larger workloads.

Stage 2 — which is currently being developed — will introduce a competition-driven environment where miners compete on specific tasks required by real clients. These tasks will extend beyond compression and upscaling and may include things like encoding, lip-syncing, context understanding, and other specialized video transformations.

Large client workloads will run inside trusted execution environments provided by third-party infrastructure providers, with miners competing to produce the best performing models and solutions for those tasks.

In this model, revenue from real workloads flows back into the subnet, increasing reward pools for valuable tasks and helping offset emissions. The goal is to create a system where capital inflows from usage exceed token outflows from emissions, strengthening the long-term economics of the subnet.

We understand concerns around miner profitability today. However, the objective is to move the subnet toward a structure where rewards come from real demand and high-value tasks, rather than distributing emissions broadly without corresponding workload.

More detailed documentation on the competition mechanism and future task structure will be shared shortly. 

We appreciate the feedback and will continue to work closely with miners as the subnet evolves.",2026-03-13T13:38:25.193000+00:00,2026-03-13T13:41:29.253000+00:00,False,,False,0,0,2
