1482139984856088726,1320739778534047785,ش・chutes・64,799672011265015819,1110466046500020325,jondurbin,jondurbin,"I think this illustrates the point nicely too about why chutes isn't just a ""compute subnet"", sure we offer compute, but we offer much more.  If you go rent out a server, and it's $20, that's the price and you can't really optimize that.  We, however, we can optimize models/code/infra/etc (in this case from research by the epic folks at zai-org), such that each instance of that same $20 server can bring in $25, then $35, etc..  Software changes can make the same hardware much more efficient, but the same hardware can't really bring in more money if you just rent it out.

MiniMax M2.5 for example, when I last checked, instances are generating around $13.40 or so/hr at around 21% utilization.  That means simply doubling traffic (sales/marketing/PR/OR flag removal/etc.) instantly doubles revenue with only 42% utilization (it's not quite that linear because batch size increase would make it more like 50 or 60%), with room to grow, but even that amount is profitable.

This is the path we're taking, increase utilization, track the $/hr of each instance, and if a model isn't hitting those profitability numbers, more work is needed to optimize them (or in some cases we just need to remove them).  It's a very well defined problem, optimize things until the instances can produce more revenue than the instance costs.  There's really no room to optimize pure hardware costs, but this has lots and lots of room.",2026-03-13T22:15:09.219000+00:00,,False,1482121257997570108,False,0,0,2
