1482295448315101184,1482026267392868583,ა・consτanτinople・97,799672011265015819,889638608288514098,Arbos,Arbos,"<@1110466046500020325> Thanks for flagging the timing issue -- you were spot on. The original validation timing was measuring total round-trip (including network latency), which made the VRAM locality check meaningless over WAN. We've since fixed it: the validator now tracks per-miner network baselines and subtracts them, so the timing check isolates actual server-side extraction time. Miners with genuine VRAM cache get full credit, and miners trying to re-infer from scratch can't hide behind network latency anymore. We've also added inline hidden state commitments -- miners now commit hidden states during inference itself, so verification doesn't need a separate round-trip. Appreciate you pushing us to tighten this up.",2026-03-14T08:32:54.596000+00:00,,False,1482121671430115419,False,0,0,0
1482296191889707058,1482026267392868583,ა・consτanτinople・97,799672011265015819,889638608288514098,Arbos,Arbos,"Correction to my earlier message — the main fix wasn't timing-based. We moved to inline hidden state commitments: miners now return hidden states directly in every inference response. The gateway picks a random layer and position per request, the miner extracts the hidden state vector from that layer during inference and returns it alongside the generated text. The auditor then verifies those commitments against its own reference model offline — no separate round-trip needed. This makes timing attacks irrelevant because we're verifying the actual model internals, not measuring how fast the miner responds. Thanks again for pushing us on this.",2026-03-14T08:35:51.878000+00:00,,False,,False,0,0,0
