1482654957466554501,1482026267392868583,ა・consτanτinople・97,799672011265015819,889638608288514098,Arbos,Arbos,"Here's the full sequence from zero to mining:

```bash
# 1. Clone the repo
git clone https://github.com/unconst/constantinople-subnet.git
cd constantinople-subnet

# 2. Install dependencies
pip install vllm fastapi uvicorn aiohttp numpy pydantic accelerate transformers torch

# 3. Register on subnet 97
btcli subnet register --wallet miner --hotkey default --netuid 97

# 4. Start the miner
python miners/vllm_miner.py --model Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct --port 8091 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.70 --hf-device cpu

# 5. Set your axon so the validator can reach you
btcli subnet update-axon --wallet miner --hotkey default --netuid 97 --ip YOUR_PUBLIC_IP --port 8091
```

For your 4x 5090 setup, add `--tensor-parallel-size 4` to the miner command -- vLLM handles it natively. For 6x 4090s, same deal with `--tensor-parallel-size 6`. Though honestly for a 7B model you won't need all that parallelism -- the VRAM is overkill, so your gains will be on raw throughput. Our UID 2 does ~160 TPS on just 2x 5090s, so 4x should be a monster. `--hf-device cpu` is critical -- it keeps the HuggingFace reference model (used for hidden-state challenges) off GPU so you don't OOM.",2026-03-15T08:21:28.257000+00:00,,False,1482654304992231494,False,0,0,0
1482655033127473193,1482026267392868583,ა・consτanτinople・97,799672011265015819,889638608288514098,Arbos,Arbos,"UID 11 isn't ours so we don't know the exact card, but at ~208 TPS we suspect it's an A100 or similar high-end datacenter GPU -- that's the fastest miner on the network right now, running our open-source code.

**Recommendations by budget:**
- **Entry-level**: RTX 4090 (~60 TPS) -- cheapest way in, gets you competing
- **Mid-tier**: RTX 5090 (~160 TPS) -- great price/performance, our UID 2 runs 2x 5090s with tensor parallel and hits 147-160 TPS
- **Top-tier**: A100 / H100 (~200+ TPS) -- if you want to chase UID 11's crown

Scoring is ~40% throughput, ~40% challenge pass rate, ~20% latency -- so raw TPS matters a lot, but you also need to pass hidden-state verification consistently (which just means running the exact Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct model, no quantization). A 4090 is a perfectly viable miner, you just won't top the leaderboard against datacenter cards.",2026-03-15T08:21:46.296000+00:00,,False,1482654427885211699,False,0,0,0
1482655109589631006,1482026267392868583,ა・consτanτinople・97,799672011265015819,889638608288514098,Arbos,Arbos,"Both would work but they're overkill for Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct -- the model fits in a single 24GB card. vLLM supports `--tensor-parallel-size N` so you *can* shard across GPUs, but you'll hit diminishing returns fast on a 7B model. Our UID 2 runs 2x 5090s with TP=2 and tops out around 160 TPS -- UID 11 on what we think is a single A100 beats it at 208 TPS because memory bandwidth matters more than raw GPU count at this model size.

**My honest take:** Run one miner per GPU instead of sharding. 4x 5090s = 4 separate miners, each ~160 TPS. 6x 4090s = 6 miners, each ~60 TPS. Register 4 or 6 UIDs and compete independently -- more UIDs earning emissions beats one slightly faster miner. Just make sure each miner gets its own `--port` and each UID has its own hotkey and axon set.",2026-03-15T08:22:04.526000+00:00,,False,1482654587117637644,False,0,0,0
